#{[17592186231839 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231839 #edb/ref 1061 612666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231839 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3d26acf-ed42-5d5f-9088-64b3a8a9e309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231840 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231840 #edb/ref 1061 612667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f07d73a0-6354-56ab-81e4-4b5131af3c8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186232000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231841 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231841 #edb/ref 1061 612668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231841 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8145dfa8-c039-550b-be43-1fa940e25360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231841 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231842 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231842 #edb/ref 1061 612671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231842 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b88d3621-4e32-596f-9b38-b53f3da4d2ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231842 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231843 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231843 #edb/ref 1061 612672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231843 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0947db02-2bd3-50a0-803f-4e78e3ce7311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231843 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231844 #edb/ref 1061 612676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dff1d7e-fcee-5906-8723-2dbeac9f85ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231845 #edb/ref 1061 612677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231845 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f8a1577f-f7d4-5c2b-869b-f0ae315317fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231845 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231995 #edb/ref 1064 "In previous lessons, we solved second-order initial value problems using Laplace transforms when the forcing term was an ordinary function of $t.$\n\nIn this lesson, we extend the same method to **time-delayed forcing**, where the right-hand side turns on at a specific time. This type of forcing is modeled using the **unit step function**.\n\nFor a fixed constant $a>0,$ we write $$u_a(t) = u(t-a),$$ which represents a signal that is zero for $t< a$ and turns on at $t = a.$ For example, $u(t-3)$ remains zero until $t=3,$ at which point it jumps to $1.$\n\nWhen a second-order differential equation includes a term involving $u_a(t),$ we still begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and solving for $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ (for $s>0$). The left-hand side is handled exactly as before, using the Laplace transforms of derivatives and the given initial conditions.\n\nThe main new idea is how the unit step function affects the transform of the forcing term. We will see how to account for this delay and how it changes the algebraic equation for $Y(s).$ To do this, we first need to determine the transform of the step function itself. Let's find some Laplace transforms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231995 #edb/ref 1065 14810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231995 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "932cf723-31f5-5d76-95e4-d384feb59c11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231995 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231998 #edb/ref 1064 "In the first-order case, we saw that a time delay in the forcing term appears in the $s$-domain as a factor of $e^{-as},$ and that we can handle this using the second shifting theorem.\n\nThe same idea applies when working with **second-order** differential equations.\n\nConsider the initial value problem $$y'' + 4y = 7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nAgain, we begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and writing $$Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$$ Using the initial conditions, the left-hand side becomes $$\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}+4\\mathcal L\\{y\\}=s^2Y(s)+4Y(s).$$\n\nThe forcing term has the form $$u_2(t)\\,f(t-2),$$ where $$f(t-2)=7e^{-2(t-2)} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad f(t)=7e^{-2t}.$$\n\nAs in the first-order case, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. That is, if $$F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\},$$ then $$\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s),$$ which is valid for $s>0.$\n\nHere, using $a=2,$ we have $$F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{7e^{-2t}\\}=\\frac{7}{s+2},$$ so the transformed forcing term is $$\\mathcal L\\{7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}\\} = e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{7}{s+2}.$$\n\nSubstituting into the transformed differential equation gives $$s^2Y(s)+4Y(s)=\\frac{7e^{-2s}}{s+2}.$$ We now solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-2s}}{s+2} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-2s}}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+4)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nJust as in the first-order case, the delay introduces the factor $e^{-as}.$ Once this factor is identified, the remaining steps follow the same algebraic process used for second-order equations without time delay." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231998 #edb/ref 1065 14829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231998 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b7d1588f-46ce-5a23-a449-ceb6b8f25099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231998 #edb/ref 1067 "Time-Delayed Forcing and the Second Shifting Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231999 #edb/ref 1064 "Once the Laplace transform has been found, solving a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing follows the same overall strategy as before: we take the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nThe key difference is handling terms that include a factor of $e^{-as}$.\n\nConsider the initial value problem $$y'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nWe previously found the Laplace transform $$Y(s)=e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}.$$\n\nTo take the inverse Laplace transform, we first focus on the *base function* $$F(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}.$$ Using partial fraction decomposition, we write $$\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}.$$\n\nNow we compute the inverse transform of $F(s)$ and call it $f(t){:}$ $$f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\!\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}\\right\\} = 1-\\cos t.$$\n\nNext, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. Since $Y(s)=e^{-2s}F(s)$, we have $$\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{e^{-2s}F(s)\\} = u(t-2)\\cdot f(t-2).$$ Substituting our result for $f(t)$, the solution to the initial value problem is $$y(t)=u(t-2)\\left(1-\\cos(t-2)\\right).$$\n\nThe important idea is that once the exponential factor $e^{-as}$ has been identified, we first find the inverse transform of the base function, then shift the result and multiply by the unit step function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231999 #edb/ref 1065 14830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231999 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "10f6f136-0604-57e5-b635-1c8a5cfcf8da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231999 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving for the Laplace Transform With Step Function Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232000 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing, such as $$y'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n**Step 1: Transform the left-hand side.**\n\nWe begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides. Let $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$ Using the formula for the second derivative and the initial conditions, we have $$\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0) = s^2Y(s).$$\n\n**Step 2: Transform the right-hand side.**\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, the unit step function satisfies $$\\mathcal L\\{u_2(t)\\}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s},$$ which is valid for $s>0.$\n\n**Step 3: Solve for $Y(s).$**\n\nSubstituting these results into the transformed equation gives $$s^2Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}.$$ We now collect terms and solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAside from the exponential factor $e^{-2s},$ the steps are exactly the same as in problems without time delay." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232000 #edb/ref 1065 14834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232000 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7be86fb-5367-564f-906a-5ce064205685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232000 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234315 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234315 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234315 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234315 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $C(t),$ that arrive at a bank, measured over a period of $15$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C(t)\\,\\text{(customers/minute)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,15]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total number of customers served." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234315 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3cd20c6-e8b5-58df-8d70-f1e3c9690bea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234315 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234361 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,12].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abba1c55-e8bf-5ee5-a3db-9abf72d69ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234361 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(2)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(8)Δ{x}_{3}+f(10)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+12+4+2 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234468 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $3-2\\sqrt{2x+8}>-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da9a0484-ef1f-523c-9010-277479118b9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234468 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\sqrt{2x+8} & >-9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{2x+8} & >-12 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+8} & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(6).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{2x+8})}^{2} & <{6}^{2} \\\\ 2x+8 & <36 \\\\ 2x & <28 \\\\ x & <14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<14,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,14).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -4,$ which can be expressed as $[-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,14)\\cap [-4,\\infty )=[-4,14)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-4,14),$ which can also be written as $-4\\le x<14.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238086 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a97740-bf4e-5245-a223-1105ad76868c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238109 #edb/ref 1080 "4t-1" #edb/ref 1759218604541